BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bobby Finger & Lindsey Weber – I Want to Be Famous
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260928T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260928T220000
DTSTAMP:20261006T001646Z
UID:funcheap-1627497@sf-cal
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:Literature
DESCRIPTION:Submitted by the Event OrganizerWhile being photographed in 19
 66\, Warhol reportedly said\, “Everyone wants to be famous.” (To which
  his photographer\, Nat Finkelstein responded\, “Yeah\, for about fiftee
 n minutes\, Andy.”) But Warhol was right then\, and he’s right now. Fi
 fteen minutes be damned\, all you need is the drive—or desperation—and
  a singular spark. But if you’re not careful\, you’ll end up a Who.\nW
 ho is a Who? In I Want to Be Famous\, Bobby Finger and Lindsey Weber\, the
  pop culture journalists behind the podcast Who? Weekly\, distill celebrit
 y into two categories—Whos and Thems—transcending the snarky\, antiqua
 ted judgment of the “A-Listers” to “D-listers.” If you come across
  an allegedly famous face you’ve never seen before and are compelled to 
 utter “Who?”\, well\, there’s your answer (Can you picture Rita Ora\
 , Ava Max\, or Hilaria Baldwin without Googling them?). If the subject eli
 cits something along the lines of\, “Oh\, Them\,” there you’ve found
  the opposite (Beyoncé\, Taylor Swift\, Tom Cruise). It’s the fundament
 al duality of fame.\n\nAnd yet\, as more Whos spawn\, the path to Themdom 
 narrows. We’ve entered an era where accessibility to fame is within ever
 yone’s grasp\, though only a select few can crack the algorithm and hold
  our ever-diminishing attention spans. Celebrities have taken desperate me
 asures to stay relevant—from the makeup\, supplements\, and alcohol they
  peddle to the Notes App apologies they post—as the media who cover them
  compete with celebrities breaking their ow...\n\n$38.00\n\nTickets: https
 ://bookpassage.com/event/bobby-finger-lindsey-weber-i-want-be-famous\nDeta
 ils: https://sf.funcheap.com/bobby-finger-lindsey-weber-famous/\nSource: F
 uncheap SF
IMAGE;VALUE=URI:https://cdn.funcheap.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Finger
 400-x-580-px.png
LAST-MODIFIED:20261006T001646Z
LOCATION:Angelico Hall\, 20 Olive Ave
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://sf.funcheap.com/bobby-finger-lindsey-weber-famous/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Yerba Buena Illuminated: Free 95-Ft Art Projection on Market Stree
 t
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260928T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260928T220000
DTSTAMP:20261006T001646Z
UID:funcheap-1633810@sf-cal
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:Art Museums
DESCRIPTION:Submitted by the Event OrganizerYerba Buena Illuminated: Free 
 95-Ft Art Projection on Market StreetSan Francisco has officially turned a
  downtown building into a giant art projector.\n\nYerba Buena Illuminated 
 launched Tuesday\, August 25\, 2026 projecting giant digital artworks onto
  the historic Humboldt Bank Building at 785 Market St.\, at the top of Yer
 ba Buena Lane. The massive projection covers a 95-foot-high by 46-foot-wid
 e section of the building\, turning one of Market Street’s historic faca
 des into a giant outdoor art screen.\n\nThe free outdoor art show runs nig
 htly from twilight until 10 p.m.\, with the first artwork on view for abou
 t 90 days. The inaugural projection is Jennifer Steinkamp’s It’s a nic
 e day for a white wedding. (2008)\, from SFMOMA’s collection. Inspired b
 y Billy Idol’s “White Wedding\,” the digital artwork covers the Beau
 x-Arts-style building with a cascade of animated red\, white and yellow we
 dding flowers.\nThe project is a collaboration between Yerba Buena Partner
 ship\, SFMOMA\, the Museum of the African Diaspora and Yerba Buena Center 
 for the Arts. And this first installation is just the beginning: MoAD and 
 YBCA will curate future projections in 2027\, so the giant building-as-can
 vas is sticking around.\nThe projection is designed to serve as a giant we
 lcome sign for the Yerba Buena arts district\, home to more than 60 public
  artworks and some of San Francisco’s biggest cultural institutions. The
  rotating projections will continue to light up the Humboldt Bank...\n\nFr
 ee\n\nTickets: https://yerbabuena.org/\nDetails: https://sf.funcheap.com/y
 erba-buena-illuminated-free-95-ft-art-projection-on-market-street-10/\nSou
 rce: Funcheap SF
IMAGE;VALUE=URI:https://cdn.funcheap.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/6avfkp
 PA.jpeg
LAST-MODIFIED:20261006T001646Z
LOCATION:Humboldt Bank Building\, 785 Market Street\, San Francisco\, CA
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://sf.funcheap.com/yerba-buena-illuminated-free-95-ft-art-project
 ion-on-market-street-10/
END:VEVENT
